--- 3. quoted extracts (verbatim, with document/section) ---
(A) TRM DS60001702S, section 3.12.12 "Watchdog":
    "Watchdog_0 is associated with the E51 core and is the only one out of the five MSS watchdogs
     capable of resetting the MSS when it triggers. Each of the other four watchdogs is maintained by
     a dedicated U54 core and is only capable of interrupting the E51 upon triggering."
(A) TRM DS60001702S, section 3.12.12.1 "Features":
    "A 32-bit timer counts down from a preset value to zero, then performs one of the following
     user-configurable operations: If the counter is not refreshed, it times out and either causes a
     system reset or generates an interrupt to the processor."
    "The watchdog timer counter is halted when the processor enters the Debug state."
    "The watchdog timer can be configured to generate a wake-up interrupt when the processor is in WFI mode."
(A) TRM DS60001702S, section 3.12.12.2.2 "32-Bit Counter":
    "The MSS watchdogs are not enabled initially when the MSS comes out of Reset. When the device is
     powered up, the watchdog timer is enabled with the timeout period set to approximately 10.47
     seconds (if VDD = 1.2 V)."
(A) TRM DS60001702S, section 3.12.12.2.3 "Timeout Detection":
    "The MSS watchdog timeout is implemented as a locked down-counter configuration. The software loads
     a start value into the WDOG_TIME register, programs the trigger threshold and periodically refreshes
     the counter before the trigger level is crossed. If the counter crosses the trigger value, a
     non-maskable timeout interrupt is asserted; if it continues to zero, a reset is generated.
     Note: Only watchdog_0 can reset the MSS. The other watchdogs can only generate interrupts to the
     E51 core."
(A) TRM DS60001702S, Figure 3-36 "WatchDog Block Diagram" signal labels: WDOGLOAD[25:0], WDOGMVRP[31:0],
    WDOGENABLE, WDOGTIMEOUT, WDOGTIMEOUTINT, WDOGWAKEUPINT, PROGRAMMING, PCLK, PORESETN, RCOSCRESETN.
(A) TRM DS60001702S, MSS peripheral address table: WDOG0 20001000, WDOG1 20101000, WDOG2 20103000,
    WDOG3 20105000, WDOG4 20107000 (each 0x1000 bytes).
(B) PU/Reset UG DS60001676M, section 4.2.2.3 "MSS E51 Processor Watchdog Timeout Reset":
    "The MSS can be configured such that the E51 processor's watchdog timer causes a reset of the MSS when
     the timer runs out."
(B) PU/Reset UG DS60001676M, section 4.2.2.3 (the reset-reason register):
    "The user can access the 32-bit register RESET_SR to know which reset caused the MSS to be reset."
(B) PU/Reset UG DS60001676M, section 4.2.1 (the external device-reset path, distinct from a watchdog reset):
    "The DEVRST_N may be pulled low by an external source in order to schedule a full device reset and reboot. This is not an asynchronous reset, but asserts a
     Non-Maskable Interrupt (NMI) to the processor in the system controller, which then starts the watchdog timer to schedule an unstoppable device reset, to be
     asserted after the firmware has disabled I/Os and fully executed a safe power-down of the fabric."
    and the associated sequence: "1. The reset signal propagates as a non-maskable interrupt to the System Controller, which first disables all the I/Os.
     2. Starts the Watchdog timer to schedule a device reset. 3. Powers down the Fabric."
(B) PU/Reset UG DS60001676M, Table 4-2 "MSS Reset Reasons":
    "WDOG_RESET  5  Watchdog  This indicates that the watchdog reset has been activated."
    (other reasons: SCB_PERIPH_RESET 0, SCB_MSS_RESET 1, SCB_CPU_RESET 2, DEBUGGER_RESET 3,
     FABRIC_RESET 4, GPIO_RESET 6, SCB_BUS_RESET 7, CPU_SOFT_RESET 8, Reserved 31:9)
(B) PU/Reset UG DS60001676M, section 4.2.2.4 "MSS Peripheral Soft Resets":
    "Each MSS peripheral has a soft reset register (SOFT_RESET_CR) bit associated with it in the MSS
     system registers and this bit must be written to "1" and then "0" to allow the peripheral to be used."
(D) Product Overview DS00005395C, section 4.5.11 "Watchdog Timer":
    "There are five watchdogs in the MSS, one per CPU. The watchdog guards against system crashes by
     requiring that it is regularly serviced by the assigned processor. The watchdog is not enabled at
     reset and generates a NMI upon reaching the trigger value. Once enabled, the watchdog cannot be
     [disabled]."
(E) Errata DS00006185A, section 1:
    "This errata applies to part number prefixes RTPF500T, RTPF500ZT, RTPFS460ZT and RTPFS160ZT."
(E) Errata DS00006185A, section 1 (device descriptions):
    "Refer to the following documents for descriptions of ES and MS devices:
     - RT PolarFire(R) RTPF500T MS Description
     - RT PolarFire(R) RTPF500ZT MS Description
     - RT PolarFire(R) SoC MS Description"
(E) Errata DS00006185A, three items only: (1) Deprecated feature - use of external VERIFY_DIGEST with
    the POR digest check for fabric components; (2) the SCSM re-entry restriction for RTPF500ZT, RTPFS460ZT
    and RTPFS160ZT; (3) a pointer to the Libero SoC software errata.
    => NO erratum concerns the MSS watchdog.

--- 4. what these documents answer, and what they do not ---
answer:   which watchdog can reset the MSS (only WDOG0/E51); the reset reason bit (WDOG_RESET, bit 5);
           the power-up default timeout (approx. 10.47 s at VDD 1.2 V) and "not enabled initially when the
           MSS comes out of Reset"; the counter semantics (32-bit down counter, NMI at the trigger
           crossing, reset at zero); the debug-halt and WFI behaviours; the five instance base addresses;
           the hardware load field width (26 bits) and MVRP width (32 bits); the peripheral soft-reset
           release mechanism; the absence of any published watchdog erratum for the flight device.
do NOT answer: the watchdog register map, reset values and design-time MSS configuration (the "PolarFire
           SoC Device Register Map" document — not retrievable here); the counter clock / prescaler value
           as a stated specification; the RT-specific "MS Description"; and every board-specific item
           (MSS configuration flash-bit defaults, mission hart and instance assignment, the mission clock
           assignment, the board reset scope) which is not a public document at all.

--- 5. labelled INFERENCE (not a fact, not an adopted parameter) ---
The TRM's "approx. 10.47 seconds" power-up default is consistent with the cited example driver's maximum
load value 16 777 200 (0xFFF3D8) at a counter clock of about 1.6026 MHz, i.e. about 410 MHz / 256 -
consistent with the header's stated prescaler of 256 but with a different AXI clock than the header's
illustrative 25 MHz example (which yields 171 s for the same tick count). If that inference holds, the
reaction-window ceiling (TRIGGER <= 4095 ticks) would be about 2.6 ms rather than the 41.9 ms computed at
25 MHz. ASSUMPTIONS: the design-time default load equals the driver's maximum value, and the prescaler is
256. NOT CONFIRMED: the counter clock and the prescaler are not stated in any retrieved document, and the
design-time default load is in the document that could not be retrieved. No value is adopted here.

